00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:06.313
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, right.

00:09:06.313 --> 00:09:09.760
Because-- and I think the
reason that this fits very well

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:12.610
is when you said this
data goes to two places,

00:09:12.610 --> 00:09:14.080
Pub/Sub is amazing
for that, right?

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:16.848
You publish it once and then you
can have multiple subscribers.

00:09:16.848 --> 00:09:17.890
DINA GRAVES PORTMAN: Yes.

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.290
Yes, multiple
subscribers, and we

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:24.010
can do a filtered subscription
so then our little function

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:25.720
doesn't have to do any logic.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:28.750
It doesn't have to say,
like, hey, if else,

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:30.370
just go here, go here.

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:32.230
We're just sending
everything to Pub/Sub

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:35.770
and then we can have our
filtered subscription that

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:40.030
will pick up whenever the match
thing doesn't happen, you know?

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:41.280
And that can trigger an email.

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:42.655
MARTIN OMANDER:
Oh, right, right.

00:09:42.655 --> 00:09:43.280
Yep, yep.

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:45.220
So we have that
filter subscription

00:09:45.220 --> 00:09:46.710
